description:
Replace source_line counter IDs with monotonic IDs per function. Emit a .covmap.json file during compilation that maps each counter_id to:
{
  "counters": [
    {"id": 0, "type": "function_entry", "fn": "main", "line": 10},
    {"id": 1, "type": "branch_then", "fn": "main", "line": 12, "branch": 0},
    {"id": 2, "type": "branch_else", "fn": "main", "line": 12, "branch": 0},
    ...
  ]
}
This decouples counter allocation from line numbers and enables branch tracking.

Every error type / struct currently writes its own `.message()` / `.to_string()` method and callers spell it out at every interpolation:

    println("error: ${err.message()}")
    println("user: ${u.to_string()}")

If `${expr}` in a string literal desugared to `${expr.display()}` (calling a stdlib `Display` trait), every type becomes interpolatable:

    println("error: ${err}")        // calls IsolatedError.display()
    println("user: ${u}")           // calls User.display()
    println("count: ${42}")         // primitives have impl Display
    println("items: ${items}")      // List<T: Display> has blanket impl

## Surface

    trait Display { fn display(self) -> string }

    // Primitive impls in core/builtin
    impl Display for int { fn display(self) -> string { int_to_string(self) } }
    impl Display for string { fn display(self) -> string { self } }
    impl Display for bool { fn display(self) -> string { if self { "true" } else { "false" } } }
    impl Display for float { fn display(self) -> string { float_to_string(self) } }

    // User types opt in:
    impl Display for IsolatedError {
        fn display(self) -> string {
            match self { .Crashed -> "child crashed", ... }
        }
    }

## Implementation

1. Add `Display` trait to `@std/core` (or wherever Result lives).
2. Define `impl Display for int / string / bool / float` in core.
3. Parser: `${expr}` in InterpString stays parsed as before.
4. Codegen: emit a `Display.display(expr)` trait dispatch instead of relying on string coercion. Today's string-coercion path stays as fallback for backwards compat.
5. Diagnostic: if T doesn't impl Display, error at the `${value}` site with a helpful "add `impl Display for T`" message.

## Acceptance

- `println("err: ${IsolatedError.Crashed}")` works.
- `println("user: ${User { id: 1, name: \"alice\" }}")` works when `impl Display for User` exists.
- Without impl: F1xxx error at the interpolation site, not a silent fallthrough.
- Every existing `${expr}` site keeps working (primitives have impls).
- Drop `.message()` from IsolatedError / MarshalError / etc. — replace with `impl Display`.

## Drives

The cheapest big win across the codebase. Every error / struct / list / map drops its bespoke to_string method. Mirrors Rust `std::fmt::Display`, Swift `CustomStringConvertible`, Go `Stringer`, Haskell `Show`.

When a @comptime fn body contains a `match` statement on its arg, eval fails with: 'eval error: match statements are not supported by the tree-walk evaluator'.

Repro:
```
@comptime
fn expand_command(s: Stmt) -> Stmt {
    match s {
        .ComponentBlock(_, instance, _, _) -> {
            return Stmt.TypeDecl("Command_" + instance, ..., ...)
        }
        _ -> return s
    }
}
```

This blocks the canonical vez6.8 macro pattern: `@expand` macros receive the wrapped Stmt and need to pattern-match to extract the instance name + body. Without match support, every macro is forced into ugly if-let-style chains or is_X(s) helper boolean checks — assuming those are supported either.

Surfaced while testing vez6.8 end-to-end per Stage 3 of GETTING_TO_CLI_V2.md.

The eval module (features/eval/mod.av or similar) handles tree-walk evaluation of @comptime bodies. It needs a Match arm in eval_stmt (or eval_expr for match-as-expression) that:
1. Evaluates the subject
2. Walks each arm, attempting to bind the pattern
3. On first matching arm, evaluates the body in extended bindings
4. On no match (and no wildcard), errors

Pattern types needed initially:
- .EnumCtor(variant_name, fields) destructuring (the most common case)
- _ wildcard
- Identifier binding (for match arms that just bind the whole subject)

Effort: 1-2 days. eval already handles most expressions; match is structurally similar — pattern compile + branch dispatch over the runtime Value. The Value type already has enum_type/enum_variant/enum_payload, so destructuring is a tag check + payload index walk.

